- loaded before cutoff, queried after cutoff: trace + load-status OK; reload attempt → 422.
- reroute: reroute bag to new leg with cutoff; load uses new leg's cutoff (new leg cutoff passed → 422; old leg cutoff irrelevant). Reroute validations: short bag 422, loaded bag 422, delivered 422, first leg origin mismatch 422.
- concurrency: cutoff update vs load — serialized by leg lock + version: exactly one wins (load at v1 then cutoff update needs v2; or cutoff first then load's expectedVersion stale → 409).
- idempotency: cutoff update replay same key → same result, version bumped once; load fingerprint includes operator/container — same requestId different container → 409.
- container: same container different leg → 422; released after arrive → reusable.
- load-status query: after load shows container/operator/cutoff.

Clock control: baggageService.setClock with mutable holder (AtomicReference<Instant>).

Note: cutoff update uses expectedVersion and bumps leg version — this affects load's expectedVersion. Tests must track versions.

Now, one thing to verify: `TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE` read as OffsetDateTime works in H2 — existing code does it for short_registered_at. Good.

Instant in request DTO: Jackson deserializes ISO strings to Instant by default in Spring Boot (JavaTimeModule registered, WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS disabled). Good.

Let me also double check H2 MySQL mode supports `TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE` — yes, existing schema uses it.

`container_occupancy.occupied_at` type TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE.

